const HQ_2=[];const OQ_2=[];const NQ_2=[];
/* ===== know: Rydin (2007) ===== */
HQ_2.push(
{t:"know",fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Rydin argues that consensus building is poorly suited to most knowledge disputes in planning. Statement II: Innes says consensus building works when information is fully shared or when uncertainty is rampant, and in both conditions the dispute is about values rather than about knowledge.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:0,e:"Rydin uses Innes's own two conditions: with fully shared information knowledge is not disputed, and with rampant uncertainty there is no certain knowledge, so in both cases deliberation is about values. Most planning lies between these extremes, which is exactly why consensus building cannot handle knowledge disputes; so II is the reason for I, not just a parallel fact."},
{t:"know",fmt:"combo",q:"Which claims does Rydin (2007) make about her knowledge types? I. None of them is value-free, because choosing what to describe or explain already involves value judgements. II. Normative knowledge of State B1 counts as knowledge because any desired future may serve as a planning goal. III. Every type carries an embedded causal story, even purely descriptive claims.",o:["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],a:2,e:"I and III are Rydin's two common features of all types (market prices vs child poverty; 'all knowledge has a causal story embedded within it'). II reverses her point: normative knowledge is knowledge precisely because 'not any imaginings will serve as a planning goal'; it must engage with possible realities."},
{t:"know",fmt:"case",q:"Hypothetical: a Dutch water board consults residents on a dike reinforcement. Residents of one polder report that their cellars flooded in each of the last three heavy rain events, although the board's groundwater model predicts low risk there. Other residents say the dike must keep its historic profile because 'it is who we are'. Following Rydin (2007), how should the planner treat these inputs?",o:["As two forms of local knowledge of equal standing, because both are rooted in residents' lived experience of the polder","As an empirical claim to be tested against the model, and a separate identity claim to be heard on its own terms","As inputs to set aside, because only the accredited groundwater model can produce valid knowledge on flood risk","As normative knowledge of State B1, because both describe a future for the dike that residents want to reach"],a:1,e:"The cellar reports claim a causal pattern in the material world, so they are an empirical knowledge claim that may legitimately challenge the expert model and must be tested (closing down). The 'who we are' statement is an emotional or identity claim, important but different. Treating both as equal 'local knowledge' is the Sandercock-style move Rydin criticises: experience is not by itself a sufficient condition for knowledge."},
{t:"know",fmt:"critique",q:"Rydin proposes arenas in which planners test and recognise knowledge claims. Which criticism of this proposal would a Marxist political economy perspective most plausibly make?",o:["Testing presumes claims can be judged against each other, but claims are valid only within their own frameworks","What passes the test reflects who owns land and capital, so arenas may entrench the developers' own framing of problems","Arenas are a costly detour; actors with clear property rights could simply bargain their way to efficient outcomes","Arenas presume knowledge can be pooled centrally, while useful knowledge is dispersed and only used by acting on it"],a:1,e:"A Marxist critique points to structural power: in a capitalist land market the expertise that counts (for example the development industry's market-led framing of the housing 'problem', which Rydin herself mentions) is backed by property and capital, so a procedural testing arena does not touch the underlying inequality. The first option is a strong relativist critique, the third a rational choice (Coasean) one and the fourth a Hayekian one."},
{t:"know",fmt:"nuance",q:"Which paraphrase states Rydin's view of local experience most precisely?",o:["Local experience is a sufficient basis for knowledge, since residents live closest to the conditions that planners regulate","Local experience may ground a knowledge claim, but experience alone does not make it knowledge; the claim must be tested","Local experience mostly expresses values and emotions, so it belongs in normative debate rather than in testing arenas","Local experience can count as knowledge only once it is translated into the vocabulary of accredited expertise"],a:1,e:"Following Collins and Evans, Rydin writes that experience 'may be an important basis for claiming local or experiential knowledge' but 'is not of itself a sufficient condition for it to be knowledge'. The first option overstates; the third understates (she calls it patronising to assume lay discourse is limited to experiential stories or emotions); the fourth contradicts her point that knowledge claims can be expressed in different languages."},
{t:"know",fmt:"excerpt",q:"A municipal consultation report states: 'We received over 2,000 comments. Every resident's view was recorded as local knowledge and weighted equally in the final plan, because those who live in the area know it best.' Which reading most directly exposes the weakness of this approach, and why?",o:["Rydin (2007): the process opens up but never closes down, so knowledge claims dissolve into the other claims residents make","Davoudi (2006): the process treats evidence as neutral input to a linear model, ignoring how ideology filters research use","Scott (1998): the process imposes a legible, simplified view from above that erases the practical knowledge of residents","Olson (Hindmoor): equal weighting invites free-riding, since each resident can count on the others to make the effort to comment"],a:0,e:"The report labels every view 'knowledge' and gives no arena for testing, which is Rydin's diagnosis: planning theory 'has tended to be better at discussing opening-up than closing-down', and without testing 'knowledge claims lose their specific character'. Davoudi is tempting because she criticises naive evidence use, but the report's problem is not a linear evidence model; it never separates knowledge from other claims."},
{t:"know",fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs Rydin's (2007) argument?",o:["Modernist consensus breaks down, so knowledge is seen as plural; theory turns to deliberation, which aims at agreeing on values and cannot sort out knowledge claims; hence testing arenas","Modernist consensus breaks down, so knowledge is seen as plural; theory turns to deliberation, which fails because lay knowledge is usually wrong; hence expert-led arenas replace participation","Modernist consensus breaks down, so knowledge is seen as plural; since all claims are then equally valid, planners should only make sure that every voice is heard, recorded and weighed","Modernist consensus breaks down, so knowledge is seen as plural; deliberation then fails because powerful interests capture it; hence knowledge returns to accredited experts"],a:0,e:"Rydin accepts plural, socially constructed knowledge but argues deliberation is oriented to values and agreement, so it cannot by itself distinguish knowledge claims; arenas for opening up and closing down follow. The second and fourth chains end in an expert monopoly she rejects (capture is only one of the general concerns), and the third is the strong relativism she explicitly avoids."},
{t:"know",fmt:"except",q:"Which statement is NOT consistent with Rydin's Table 2 on testing knowledge claims?",o:["Predictive claims tend to be expert-led, because lay knowledge is rooted in past and present experience","For process claims the planner can be the object of research and, in action research, also a participant","For normative claims the planner should narrow debate to the one future that the evidence shows is likeliest","For empirical claims, forums should let lay and expert perspectives examine each other's claims and resolve them"],a:2,e:"For normative claims Rydin wants informed public debate in which the planner checks that futures are genuinely possible and 'prevents any possible futures being ruled out of consideration', adding an ethical voice. Narrowing to the likeliest future confuses normative knowledge (desired B1) with predictive knowledge (expected B). The other three statements paraphrase Table 2 and the text."},
{t:"know",fmt:"who",q:"Which course author would endorse this claim: 'Knowledge claims are not more important than ethical claims, but a planning process must be able to tell them apart and give each its own arena'?",o:["Rydin (2007), on planning knowledge","Davoudi (2006), on evidence-based planning","Hayek (1944), on planning and democracy","Rydin (2021), on the governmental model"],a:0,e:"Rydin (2007) insists that knowledge claims are 'fundamentally different' from ethical claims without being more important, and builds her arenas on that distinction. Davoudi is the tempting distractor, but her concern is how evidence competes with ideology, interests and institutions in policy, not separating claim types; the governmental model treats knowledge as positively gathered by experts and puts values outside the process altogether."},
{t:"know",fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: In Rydin's Figure 1, planning is treated as standing outside the societal, economic and environmental processes that turn State A into State B. Statement II: Rydin argues that planning has real effects on the physical environment even when it fails to reach its stated goal B1.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:3,e:"Rydin explicitly says Figure 1 does not place planning outside these processes: 'on the contrary, it is clearly constituted by such processes'; the figure only isolates planning's particular contribution. II is true: she rejects the view that planning is purely symbolic and therefore adds State B2, the outcome planning actually produces."}
);
NQ_2.push(
{t:"know",fmt:"recall",q:"What, according to Rydin (2007), distinguishes knowledge from information and data?",o:["Knowledge is produced by accredited experts in institutions that guarantee its objectivity","Knowledge specifies a causal relationship, such as how an impact follows from an action","Knowledge is information that all stakeholders have agreed on through deliberation","Knowledge is data that has been checked and verified through statistical analysis"],a:1,e:"Rydin: 'the specification of a causal relationship is central to knowledge'; planners need to know how impacts follow from actions. Expert production is the old modernist view she moves away from, and agreement is a deliberative criterion, not her definition."},
{t:"know",fmt:"recall",q:"Rydin links knowledge claims to Habermas's validity claims. Which validity claim corresponds to a knowledge claim?",o:["Normative legitimacy, raised through regulatives","Truthfulness, raised through expressives","Truth, raised through constative speech acts","Comprehensibility, raised through all speech acts"],a:2,e:"Habermas distinguishes claims to truth (constatives), normative legitimacy (regulatives) and truthfulness (expressives); knowledge claims about causal relations are claims to truth, justified with reference to factual material. Normative legitimacy is the home of ethical claims."},
{t:"know",fmt:"define",q:"Rydin places planners' co-production of knowledge in line with 'Mode 2' knowledge production (Gibbons et al.). What does Mode 2 mean?",o:["Knowledge produced in universities first and then applied by practitioners","Knowledge produced only through randomised tests run by planning agencies","Knowledge produced by lay people alone, without input from any experts","Knowledge produced with practitioners and users actively taking part"],a:3,e:"Mode 2 is a mode of knowledge production 'in which practitioners and users are actively involved in the production of knowledge'. The first option describes the traditional (Mode 1) model, and the third swaps one monopoly for another, which Rydin does not propose."},
{t:"know",fmt:"example",q:"In Rydin's housing illustration of Figure 1, what is State B?",o:["The anticipated housing shortage that would arise without planning","The planned match between housing supply and housing need","Profitable developments alongside continued overcrowding and homelessness","The current state of the housing market used as a benchmark"],a:0,e:"B is where processes lead without planning (the anticipated shortage); B1 is the planned match of supply and need; B2 is the actual result (profitable developments with continued overcrowding, housing stress and homelessness); A is the current state."},
{t:"know",fmt:"inverse",q:"Which of these is a process knowledge claim in Rydin's typology (Table 1, housing market example)?",o:["Indicators of current housing supply and demand, including prices and homelessness","Trends in demographics, housebuilding and prices for the coming decade","A vision of how housing need in the locality should be met","How planning's release of housing land affects prices and need"],a:3,e:"Process knowledge explains how one state turns into another, including how planning itself works (A to B1, A to B2). Current indicators are empirical, trends are predictive, and a vision of how need should be met is normative."},
{t:"know",fmt:"not",q:"Rydin cites Sandercock's 'epistemology of multiplicity'. Which is NOT one of the ways of knowing Sandercock lists?",o:["Knowing through dialogue","Knowing from experience","Knowing by statistical modelling","Reading symbolic, non-verbal evidence"],a:2,e:"Sandercock lists dialogue, experience, local knowledge, reading symbolic and non-verbal evidence, and contemplative or appreciative knowledge. Statistical modelling is the kind of expert knowledge her list was meant to complement, not part of it."},
{t:"know",fmt:"apply",q:"A province commissions a demographic bureau to forecast housing demand to 2040. According to Rydin's Table 2, what is the planner's main role for this predictive claim?",o:["Facilitator who helps residents put their own experience into a forecast","Client of the researchers, while staying alert to relevant lay input","Mediator of a public debate in which every possible future is kept open","Participant in action research on the planning organisation's own work"],a:1,e:"Predictive claims are mostly expert-led, so the planner tends towards a consultancy model as client of the researcher, 'but one alert to the potential contribution of lay experiences'. Mediating debate with an ethical voice belongs to normative claims; action research belongs to process claims."},
{t:"know",fmt:"contrast",q:"Rydin describes three versions of the 'new orthodoxy'. Which pairing is correct?",o:["Innes: consensus is inherent in the act of communication between stakeholders","Healey: consensus must be won through negotiation and mediation","Sandercock: empowering the disadvantaged, not consensus at any price","Healey: an epistemology of knowing through dialogue and experience"],a:2,e:"Rydin: consensus building (Innes) wins consensus by negotiation and mediation; collaborative planning (Healey) sees consensus as potentially inherent in communication; radical planning (Sandercock) aims at empowerment of the most disadvantaged rather than consensus at any price. The epistemology of multiplicity is Sandercock's, not Healey's."},
{t:"know",fmt:"recall",q:"Why, according to Rydin, has contemporary planning theory blurred the line between knowledge claims and other claims?",o:["It focused on values and agreement, and labelled community claims knowledge to raise their status","It relied on positivist methods that cannot recognise lay claims as knowledge of any kind","It assumed planners hold all relevant knowledge, so the claims of others were treated only as values","It merged planning with science studies, which reject the very idea of separate validity claims"],a:0,e:"Rydin gives two reasons: an emphasis on values and how to reach agreement, and 'a tendency to label all the claims of local communities in particular as forms of knowledge', which has an emancipatory political rationale because it raises their status. The other options describe the modernist position she contrasts with."},
{t:"know",fmt:"example",q:"Rydin uses a cultural heritage example to show that…",o:["a community's historic knowledge differs from its attachment and political claims","expert heritage assessments should always override the local accounts of an area's history","heritage values are purely aesthetic, so they can never be the subject of knowledge claims","local communities lack historic knowledge, so heritage planning must remain expert-led"],a:0,e:"In the heritage example a community may hold historic knowledge useful for planning, alongside emotional attachment and political claims based on its historic stake; the knowledge claims 'are distinct from the emotional and historic claims'. She does not rank expert over local accounts or deny lay historic knowledge."}
);
OQ_2.push(
{t:"know",fmt:"short",pts:5,q:"Explain what Rydin (2007) means by 'closing down' and why she argues that planning theory has neglected it.",model:"Closing down is the second half of planners' co-production of knowledge: after opening up (giving voice to the various actors with knowledge claims, with attention to power differences), planners test claims and recognise some of them as knowledge relevant to a decision, aiming at robust knowledge (Nowotny's 'socially robust knowledge'). Rydin says theory 'has tended to be better at discussing opening-up than closing-down' because the communicative 'new orthodoxy' focused on values and agreement, and labelled all community claims as knowledge for emancipatory reasons. Yet experience alone is not a sufficient condition for knowledge, and without testing, knowledge claims dissolve into ethical, aesthetic or political claims.",rubric:["Defines closing down as testing and recognising knowledge claims","Links it to opening up (giving voice) as two parts of co-production","Gives at least one reason for the neglect (focus on values/agreement or labelling all community claims as knowledge)","States the consequence: knowledge claims lose their specific character without testing"]},
{t:"know",fmt:"excerpt",pts:15,q:"Excerpt from Rydin (2007): 'This suggests that the planning system should be conceptualised as a series of arenas in which a variety of knowledges engage with each other, with planners not just responsible for procedural aspects of the engagement but more actively involved in the co-generation of knowledge through testing and recognising knowledge claims. [...] Not all claims within planning can be recast as knowledge claims just to promote the status of the claim-maker. There is a need to assert the value of knowledge within planning alongside the value of hearing diverse stakeholders.' (a) Which epistemological position grounds this passage? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a quote from the excerpt or the article. (10)",model:"(a) A realist constructivist (soft relativist) position on knowledge: post-positivist in accepting that knowledge is plural, socially constructed and held by many actors (pluralist, situated), yet realist in holding that claims can be tested and some stand up better than others. It deliberately sits between the modernist expert monopoly and the communicative 'new orthodoxy'.\n(b) Argument 1, pluralism and co-production: knowledge is not the planner's property; planning is 'a series of arenas in which a variety of knowledges engage with each other', and planners take part in 'the co-generation of knowledge'. This rejects the modernist view of the planner as 'the knower' and fits Mode 2 production. Argument 2, testing against relativism: 'Not all claims within planning can be recast as knowledge claims just to promote the status of the claim-maker.' Rydin says knowledge is 'both socially constructed and the result of an engagement with material reality', which she calls 'realist constructivism' (Wynne). Claims must be tested and recognised (closing down), so situated does not mean that anything goes. This is also why the passage asserts the value of knowledge 'alongside' hearing stakeholders: voice and testing are complementary, not rivals. Together the quotes show a position that is pluralist about who produces knowledge but realist about judging it.",rubric:["(a) Names realist constructivism / soft relativism, or 'pluralist and situated but realist' on the course axes","(a) Places it between modernist (expert) and communicative (all voices are knowledge) planning","Argument 1 on plural knowledges/co-production with a fitting verbatim quote","Argument 2 on testing claims / not all claims are knowledge with a fitting verbatim quote","Explains how each quote shows the position rather than only quoting"]},
{t:"know",fmt:"compare",pts:10,q:"Compare Rydin (2007) with the governmental model described in Rydin (2021) on one point: how knowledge enters the planning process and who is entitled to produce it.",model:"In the governmental model (Rydin 2021) knowledge 'can be gathered and made available to the planning process in a positive way': data on the current state, forecasts and evidence on impacts are collected by experts and fed into a stepwise process (Lichfield's data collection, analysis and forecasting). Planners and the researchers they commission are the authorised producers; stakeholders are involved 'only in an instrumental way to increase effectiveness', and legitimacy comes from 'the rationality of the analysis' by planners. On the course axes this is universal, monist and realist.\nRydin (2007) starts from the breakdown of this modernist consensus: knowledge is socially constructed and held by many actors, lay and expert, so planners are co-producers rather than holders of knowledge. Knowledge enters as claims that must first be given voice (opening up) and then tested and recognised (closing down) in arenas that differ by type (empirical, process, predictive, normative). She is pluralist and situated, but keeps a realist element: claims can be tested. Both share a commitment to the value of knowledge for planning and both still give the planner a central role; the difference is that in 2007 the planner's authority comes from organising testing, not from possessing knowledge.",rubric:["Governmental model: knowledge gathered 'in a positive way' by experts; planners central; stakeholders instrumental","Rydin 2007: knowledge as claims from many actors; opening up and closing down","Uses the course knowledge axes (universal/monist vs situated/pluralist) correctly","Notes a similarity (value of knowledge, central planner role) or nuance (Rydin 2007 still realist)"]},
{t:"know",fmt:"apply",pts:10,q:"Hypothetical: in a Dutch gas-extraction region, residents report new cracks in their houses after small earthquakes and say they no longer feel safe. The operator's seismic model says risks are acceptable, a residents' group demands that extraction stop because 'it is unjust that we carry the burden for the whole country', and the ministry forecasts future earthquake frequency. Analyse this situation with Rydin's (2007) concepts and say how a planner should organise the handling of these claims.",model:"Rydin would first separate the claims. The residents' crack reports are empirical knowledge claims in lay form (experiential knowledge): they claim a causal link between quakes and damage and may legitimately challenge the expert model, as in her example of local ill-health episodes challenging risk assessments. The operator's model is an expert process and predictive claim, and the ministry's forecast is a predictive claim, which tends to be expert-led. 'It is unjust that we carry the burden' is an ethical claim and feeling unsafe is an expressive claim: important, but not knowledge claims, and they should not be relabelled as such. The planner should open up (give voice, support the less resourced residents' group) and then close down in different arenas: a forum where lay and expert empirical claims examine each other (for example joint damage inspections), a debate between rival causal models for the process and predictive claims in which the planner decides whether a model is robust enough, and a public-sphere debate on the normative question of whether extraction should continue, where the planner keeps possible futures on the table. Testing is not ranking: the ethical claim may well decide the outcome, but on its own grounds.",rubric:["Classifies at least three claims correctly (empirical lay, predictive/process expert, ethical/expressive)","Explains that experience can ground but not by itself make knowledge","Describes opening up (voice, support for weaker actors) and closing down (testing) concretely","Assigns different arenas/planner roles per type (Table 2)","Notes that knowledge claims are not more important than ethical ones"]}
);
/* ===== hayek: Hayek (1944) ===== */
HQ_2.push(
{t:"hayek",fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Hayek holds that delegating law-making power, for example to local authorities, is in itself a threat to freedom. Statement II: Hayek objects to delegation when a matter cannot be regulated by general rules but only by discretion in particular cases.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:3,e:"Hayek explicitly says 'it is not the delegation of law-making power as such, which is so objectionable', and that general rules may well be laid down by local rather than central authorities; opposing delegation as such is opposing 'a symptom instead of the cause'. II is his actual objection: discretion 'judging the case on its merits' produces what are in effect arbitrary decisions."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Hayek argues that under planning, parliaments come to be seen as ineffective 'talking shops'. Statement II: Hayek attributes this to the incompetence of individual representatives and the weakness of parliamentary institutions.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:2,e:"I is in the chapter: the inability of assemblies to carry out the mandate to plan makes them look like talking shops. But Hayek says 'the fault is neither with the individual representatives nor with parliamentary institutions as such, but with the contradictions inherent in the task': they are asked to produce agreement on everything, which majority decision cannot do."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"combo",q:"Which of these claims are made in 'Planning and democracy'? I. Individualism rests on the assumption that people are, and ought to be, selfish. II. The probability that people agree on a course of action falls as the scope of that action widens. III. People agree most easily on common ends that are means capable of serving many different purposes.",o:["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],a:1,e:"II and III are Hayek's account of the limits of common action (pp. 44–45). I is precisely what he denies: individualism 'does not assume, as is often asserted, that man is egoistic or selfish, or ought to be'; it starts from the limits of any mind's view of others' needs."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"case",q:"Hypothetical: a ministry gives a flood-defence agency one fixed goal (meet the legal safety norm for every dike) and lets it choose the means. It also creates a 'liveability agency' to 'improve well-being' by allocating funds between housing, nature, jobs and mobility. Using Hayek's chapter, which reading is most precise?",o:["Both agencies are equally dangerous, since any delegation of law-making power to experts is a step towards dictatorship","The liveability agency is acceptable because it pursues the general welfare, which every citizen agrees is highly desirable","The flood agency is the dangerous one, because highly technical tasks are where experts can best hide political choices","The flood agency is like the general with one goal; the liveability agency must rank competing ends and impose its own"],a:3,e:"Hayek contrasts the general, who has 'only one supreme goal', with the economic planner, who must choose between 'conflicting or competing ends' and so will 'impose their scale of preferences on the community'. Delegating means for an agreed end is not his target; 'general welfare' is exactly the vague term that conceals disagreement, and he rejects the idea that delegation as such is the problem."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"critique",q:"Which criticism of Hayek's chapter would a communicative or collaborative planning theorist (Healey, Innes) most plausibly make?",o:["Formal equality of rights conceals the material inequality that private ownership of the means of production keeps creating","Hayek still assumes a benevolent state; real officials pursue their own interests, so the limits on power he proposes are too weak","Hayek treats agreement as something that either already exists or not, while deliberation can build a shared understanding over time","Hayek ignores that a competitive market is itself a planned order that needs an expert state to correct market failures"],a:2,e:"Communicative theory holds that consensus can be produced through dialogue (negotiation in Innes, communication in Healey), so the 'fields where true agreement exists' are not fixed in advance. The first option is a Marxist critique, the second a public choice one (and misreads Hayek, who is already wary of officials' power), and the fourth a welfare-economics (governmental model) line."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"nuance",q:"Which paraphrase states Hayek's claim about planning and dictatorship most precisely?",o:["Dictatorship must inevitably extirpate all freedom, so any form of planning will destroy every kind of liberty","Democracy is the highest political end in itself, so any planning that weakens democracy is wrong as such","Planning leads to dictatorship chiefly because the people drawn to planning jobs tend to be hungry for power","Planning leads to dictatorship as dictatorship is the most effective tool of the coercion planning needs"],a:3,e:"Hayek: 'Our point, however, is not that dictatorship must inevitably extirpate freedom, but rather that planning leads to dictatorship because dictatorship is the most effective instrument of coercion and the enforcement of ideals'. He rejects Acton's view applied to democracy (it is 'a utilitarian device'), and his mechanism is structural, not about planners' personalities."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"excerpt",q:"A municipal policy note argues: 'The council will not decide land uses plot by plot. It adopts a few general rules that apply equally to all owners, knowing it can neither foresee nor agree on every future use, and it accepts that the resulting pattern will partly be left to chance.' Which perspective grounds this note, and why?",o:["The governmental model: politicians set clear goals and expert planners pick the most efficient means to reach them","Lindblom's incrementalism: ends and means are chosen together through small, repeated comparisons of options","Hayek's liberalism: conscious control is limited to fields of true agreement, and power is bound by general rules","Scott's high modernism: a legible, standardised order is imposed on the city from above through uniform rules"],a:2,e:"The note echoes 'the price of democracy': conscious control is 'restricted to the fields where true agreement exists, and... in some fields things must be left to chance', and it prefers general rules over discretion in particular cases. Scott is tempting because of 'uniform rules', but high modernism designs a complete visual order; this note deliberately does not design the outcome."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs Hayek's argument?",o:["People agree there must be a plan but not on its ends; the assembly cannot agree on one by majority, so it delegates to experts, who rank ends by their own preferences","People agree there must be a plan but not on its ends; the assembly delegates to experts because the task is too technical, so only technical detail leaves parliament's control","People agree there must be a plan and also on its ends; the assembly votes the plan clause by clause, so the separate parts gradually add up to one coherent whole","People agree there must be a plan but not on its ends; capitalists then capture the assembly, so planning ends up serving the interests of property owners"],a:0,e:"Hayek's chain runs from agreement on the mechanism only, through parliament's inability to agree on a unitary plan, to delegation, experts imposing their scale of preferences, separate plans that do not add up, and the cry for an economic dictator. He explicitly rejects technical complexity as the root cause, says a plan cannot be voted clause by clause, and the capture story is Marxist, not his."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"except",q:"Which statement is NOT consistent with 'Planning and democracy'?",o:["When public authorities control over half of national income, they indirectly control almost all economic life","Hitler did not have to destroy democracy; he merely took advantage of a democracy that had already decayed","Democracy is valuable as a means to safeguard internal peace and individual freedom, not as an end in itself","As civilisation advanced, moral rules grew more numerous and detailed, making comprehensive plans easier"],a:3,e:"Hayek says the opposite: civilisation has been accompanied by 'a steady diminution of the sphere in which individual actions are bound by fixed rules'; moral rules became fewer and more general, and a code complete enough for a unitary plan would reverse this. The other three statements paraphrase the Germany 1928 example, the remark on Hitler, and his view of democracy as a 'utilitarian device'."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"who",q:"Which course author would endorse this claim from the lecture: 'A theory can only define a kind of pattern; how the pattern actually turns out depends on circumstances we cannot fully know, so we should stop trying to steer phenomena to a desired outcome'?",o:["Marx, on estranged labour and class","Davoudi, on evidence-based planning","Hayek, on the theory of complex phenomena","Rydin, on the governmental planning model"],a:2,e:"The HC4 slide presents this as Hayek's (1989) 'theory of complex phenomena' and contrasts it with Marx's determinism (note: it is from the lecture, not from 'Planning and democracy'). The governmental model assumes the opposite, that evidence lets planners choose the best means, and Davoudi criticises naive evidence use without rejecting steering as such."}
);
NQ_2.push(
{t:"hayek",fmt:"recall",q:"How does Hayek describe the common feature of all collectivist systems, communism and fascism included?",o:["The abolition of private ownership of the means of production","The rule of a single party claiming to speak for the whole people","The replacement of markets by the decisions of trained experts","Organising society's labours deliberately for one social goal"],a:3,e:"Hayek takes the socialist phrase 'the deliberate organisation of the labours of society for a definite social goal' as what all collectivisms share; they differ only in the goal. Abolishing private property is Marx's programme, not Hayek's definition of collectivism."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"recall",q:"Why, according to Hayek, can 'the common good' or 'general welfare' not guide a single economic plan?",o:["Welfare depends on countless things in endless combinations, so it has no single scale","Most citizens are selfish, so they will never accept a goal that serves others' welfare","Welfare can only be measured in money, and planners have no access to real market prices","These goals are set by politicians, who lack the expertise to translate them into plans"],a:0,e:"Hayek: 'The welfare and the happiness of millions cannot be measured on a single scale of less and more'; it could only be expressed as a complete hierarchy of ends, a 'complete ethical code', which does not exist. He explicitly denies that the argument depends on selfishness."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"example",q:"What did Professor Laski propose, according to Hayek's chapter?",o:["Replacing parliament with a council of economic experts","A referendum on each major chapter of the national plan","Legislating by broad enabling acts and Orders in Council","Leaving planning to local authorities bound by general rules"],a:2,e:"Laski proposed confining the House of Commons to airing grievances and debating general principles, with bills as 'general formulae conferring wide powers' exercised by Order in Council. Hayek uses this to show that socialists knew democratic procedure obstructs planning."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"example",q:"Hayek cites Halévy's 'composite photograph' of Percy, Mosley and Cripps to illustrate…",o:["how British socialists had already accepted delegated legislation","the cry for an economic dictator as a stage towards planning","how parliament became a talking shop unable to adopt any plan","the decay of German democracy well before Hitler took power"],a:1,e:"Halévy suggested that a composite photograph of these men would show one shared feature: the belief that 'we cannot get out of it except under some kind of dictatorial leadership'. Hayek calls 'the cry for an economic dictator' a characteristic stage in the movement towards planning."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"inverse",q:"Hayek's example of Germany in 1928, where public authorities controlled about 53 per cent of national income, is used to show that…",o:["democracy had already broken down there years before 1933","planning succeeds only in countries with a strong central state","German voters had chosen central planning by a large majority","past a certain share, the state indirectly controls nearly all of it"],a:3,e:"The 53 per cent figure illustrates that once the communal sector exceeds a certain proportion, 'the effects of its actions dominate the whole system' and the state 'indirectly controls almost the whole economic life'. The breakdown of democracy before 1933 is a separate point made later in the chapter."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"not",q:"Which is NOT something Hayek attributes to the liberal creed or a competitive system?",o:["It guarantees citizens a broadly equal material standard of living","It reduced the range of subjects on which agreement is necessary","It restricted government to fields where majority agreement is possible","Only within a competitive system based on private property is democracy possible"],a:0,e:"Hayek credits the liberal creed with shrinking the range of necessary agreement and argues democracy is possible only within 'a competitive system based on free disposal over private property'. Material equality is the Marxist value the lecture contrasts with Hayek's equality of rights."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"apply",q:"Civil law is as technical as economic planning, yet nobody proposes delegating it to experts. What is Hayek's explanation?",o:["Judges already act as experts, so civil law is delegated in practice","Civil law changes far too slowly for delegation to make any difference","Civil law consists of general rules on which majorities agree","Civil law concerns private parties only, so the state need not plan it"],a:2,e:"Hayek: in civil law 'legislation does not go beyond general rules on which true majority agreement can be achieved', while in directing economic activity the interests are too divergent for agreement. So technicality is not the real reason for delegation; lack of agreement is."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"define",q:"What does Hayek mean by 'plebiscitarian dictatorship'?",o:["Rule by a council of experts whose decisions are later approved by parliament","A leader confirmed from time to time by a popular vote that he can steer","A system in which every clause of the national plan is put to a referendum","A one-party state that has formally abolished all elections and parliament"],a:1,e:"It is the end state of democratic planning: parliament can at best choose persons with practically absolute power, and the head of government is 'from time to time confirmed in his position by popular vote' while having the means to make sure the vote goes his way. Elections remain, which is why it is not the same as abolishing them."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"contrast",q:"The HC4 lecture contrasts the implicit values of Marx and Hayek. Which pairing is correct?",o:["Marx: freedom through private property; Hayek: equality via shared means of production","Marx: equality of rights; Hayek: material equality as the basis for spontaneous order","Marx: liberty as the highest political end; Hayek: equality as the precondition of democracy","Marx: material equality via shared means of production; Hayek: liberty via equal rights"],a:3,e:"The slide pairs Marx with material equality through shared means of production and Hayek with liberty grounded in formal equality (equality of rights), the condition under which spontaneous order emerges. The other options swap or garble these values."},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"apply",q:"The HC4 slide pairs Hayek with Glaeser and Gyourko (2003) on zoning. What would a Hayekian planner take from their argument?",o:["Lower the 'zoning tax' on new building and set a level framework instead of steering","Build more subsidised units, since a few extra homes will bring prices down substantially","Tighten zoning, because strict rules protect home values and therefore individual freedom","Replace zoning with a national plan that matches the housing supply to measured need"],a:0,e:"Glaeser and Gyourko found zoning strictness correlates with high prices, a few subsidised units barely move prices, while cutting the 'zoning tax' could have a massive impact. The liberal planner sets framework conditions rather than limiting supply or steering outcomes (with their caveat that zoning's benefits were not considered)."}
);
OQ_2.push(
{t:"hayek",fmt:"excerpt",pts:15,q:"Excerpt from Hayek (1944): 'It is the price of democracy that the possibilities of conscious control are restricted to the fields where true agreement exists, and that in some fields things must be left to chance. But in a society which for its functioning depends on central planning, this control cannot be made dependent on a majority being able to agree; it will often be necessary that the will of a small minority be imposed upon the people, because this minority will be the largest group able to agree among themselves on the question at issue.' (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this passage? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a quote from the excerpt or the chapter. (10)",model:"(a) Liberalism (classical liberal individualism in the Austrian tradition): the individual is the judge of his own ends, collective action is legitimate only where individual ends coincide, and state power must be limited and bound by general rules.\n(b) Argument 1, value pluralism and the limits of agreement: there is no 'complete ethical code' ranking all ends, and since 'scales of value can exist only in individual minds', only partial, inconsistent scales exist. Common action must therefore be limited: 'conscious control' only 'where true agreement exists'. Where planning demands more agreement than exists, the will of 'a small minority' is imposed; this is the liberal fear of coercion, not a defence of majority rule as such. Argument 2, limitation rather than source of power: Hayek does not trust democratic origin to make power safe: 'it is not the source but the limitation of power which prevents it from being arbitrary'. He prefers general rules to discretion in particular cases ('judging the case on its merits'), and sees 'the great merit of the liberal creed' in reducing the range of subjects needing agreement, so 'things must be left to chance' rather than steered. Both arguments show an individualist, anti-collectivist and rule-of-law position rather than a structural (Marxist) or technocratic (governmental) one.",rubric:["(a) Names liberalism / classical liberal individualism (Hayek, Austrian tradition)","Argument 1 on limits of agreement / no complete ethical code, with a fitting quote","Argument 2 on limiting power by general rules (source vs limitation), with a fitting quote","Explains how each quote reveals the perspective","Distinguishes from at least one other perspective (e.g. Marxist or governmental)"]},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"short",pts:5,q:"Explain why Hayek insists that his individualism does not assume that people are selfish, and what follows from it for collective action.",model:"Hayek bases individualism not on egoism but on a cognitive limit: 'it is impossible for any man to survey more than a limited field, to be aware of the urgency of more than a limited number of needs'. Whether someone is egoistic or altruistic is 'of minor importance'; even an altruist can include only a sector of society's needs in his scale of values, so only partial and often inconsistent scales exist. Hence the individual should be 'the ultimate judge of his ends' within defined limits. For collective action this means it is legitimate only where individual ends coincide, often on multi-purpose means, and agreement shrinks as the scope of state action grows.",rubric:["Grounds individualism in the limits of knowledge/imagination, not selfishness","Notes egoism vs altruism is of minor importance","States the individual as ultimate judge of his ends","Draws the consequence: common action limited to fields of agreement (often means)"]},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"position",pts:10,q:"Place Hayek's 'Planning and democracy' on the course dimension deliberate ↔ emergent change, and justify your position with the text. Indicate where the lecture's portrayal of Hayek goes beyond this chapter.",model:"Hayek sits towards the emergent end. The chapter rejects 'the deliberate organisation of the labours of society for a definite social goal', which is how he defines collectivism, because no 'complete ethical code' exists to rank all ends and no mind can survey more than a limited field of needs. Conscious control should be 'restricted to the fields where true agreement exists', and 'in some fields things must be left to chance'. Order in the remaining fields comes from individuals following their own values within general rules, not from a plan. He is not purely emergent, though: the state is a legitimate 'person' acting deliberately where people agree (often on multi-purpose means), and he accepts deliberate rule-making, even delegated to local authorities, as long as rules are general. So: deliberate framework, emergent outcomes.\nThe lecture goes further: it attributes to Hayek 'spontaneous order' (an order emerging from the unintended consequences of many individual decisions), indeterminism and the 'theory of complex phenomena' (Hayek 1989), and links him to dispersed knowledge (Hayek 1945). None of these terms appears in this chapter, whose argument runs through value pluralism and the politics of majority rule and delegation. The positions are consistent, but a quote supporting 'spontaneous order' cannot be taken from this text.",rubric:["Places Hayek towards emergent, with nuance (deliberate general rules, state acts where agreement exists)","Supports with at least two accurate references/quotes from the chapter","Notes that spontaneous order/complex phenomena/dispersed knowledge come from the lecture and other Hayek works","Uses the dimension correctly (how change comes about), not confusing it with structure-agency"]},
{t:"hayek",fmt:"compare",pts:10,q:"Compare Hayek (1944) and Cozzolino (2020) on one point: what kind of rules public authorities should make, and why.",model:"Both prefer general rules to steering outcomes. Hayek argues that delegation of 'the power to make general rules' is acceptable, but discretion in 'the decision of particular cases' ('judging the case on its merits') creates arbitrary power; and since agreement exists only in limited fields, the price of democracy is that 'things must be left to chance'. His reason is political and moral: value pluralism, the limits of agreement, and the danger of arbitrary, coercive power.\nCozzolino translates this into urban form. He contrasts the proscriptive rules of the traditional city ('thou shalt not'), which only prevent negative externalities on neighbours and so allow spontaneous, unique configurations, with modern prescriptive rules (minimum road widths, parking and green standards, rules that coincide with the built form) that freeze adaptation. His reason is more epistemic and evolutionary: adaptability uses 'dispersed local knowledge' (citing Hayek 1945, not the 1944 chapter) and 'locality knowledge' (Jacobs), and we 'cannot predict all future practical needs'. The difference: Hayek's rules protect freedom and democracy at the level of the national economy; Cozzolino's protect the capacity of neighbourhoods to adapt over time, and he accepts top-down design of a public 'skeleton' of streets and common spaces.",rubric:["Hayek: general rules acceptable, discretion in particular cases is arbitrary; reason = limits of agreement/power","Cozzolino: proscriptive vs prescriptive rules; reason = adaptability, dispersed/locality knowledge","Identifies a genuine difference in rationale or scale","Accurate source handling (Cozzolino cites Hayek 1945, not this chapter) or mentions the public skeleton"]}
);
/* ===== cozz: Cozzolino (2020) ===== */
HQ_2.push(
{t:"cozz",fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Cozzolino notes that old historic quarters sometimes show the typical problems of an anti-adaptive neighbourhood. Statement II: Preservation policies and restrictive rules can freeze adaptation, whatever way a place was originally built.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:0,e:"In his final remarks Cozzolino says that 'due to preservation policies and restrictive rules' historic quarters 'sometimes present the typical problems of an AAN'. II gives exactly that cause: anti-adaptiveness depends on the space rules and design control leave for future action, not on age or form."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Cozzolino argues that participatory design and city gaming are enough to avoid new anti-adaptive neighbourhoods. Statement II: Cozzolino observes that such processes often still yield rational, mechanical designs that are fixed in space.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:3,e:"Cozzolino calls these practices 'remarkable' but says 'the results seem to remain questionable': they produce 'more complex design solutions' but 'not necessarily spaces that provide room for self-organizing complexity to emerge'. So I is false and II is his actual reason for doubt."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"combo",q:"For which AAN characteristics does Cozzolino say planning has DIRECT responsibility? I. Pushing large-scale developments. II. Rules that freeze the adaptation of the built form. III. The mono-functional, dormitory character of the area.",o:["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],a:0,e:"Planning is directly responsible for pushing large-scale developments, demanding large public open spaces (standards) and rules that freeze adaptation. For functions it 'can only create certain conditions': it cannot force anyone to run a business in an AAN, so III is an indirect responsibility."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"case",q:"Hypothetical: in a 1970s Dutch estate, the municipality revises the zoning plan so that dormers, extra storeys and ground-floor workshops are allowed. Two years later almost nothing has changed: every home is rented from one housing corporation, whose board must approve each alteration. Which point from Cozzolino fits most precisely?",o:["Communal condominium ownership creates an anti-commons, because any change needs a large majority of co-owners","Rule changes alone may fail, because the ownership pattern concentrates design control in very few hands","Planning rules still coincide with the built form, so density and heights remain frozen at their initial level","Attractiveness declines over time, so no external actor has a reason to invest in the area any longer"],a:1,e:"Cozzolino warns that changing rules is necessary but 'not sufficient': 'the way in which the ownership system is organized in AANs can have much higher importance and stifle their self-regenerating capacity'; 'everything depends on the pattern of ownership'. The anti-commons option is tempting but describes many co-owners needing majorities, not one landlord, and the rules here were already changed."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"critique",q:"Which criticism of Cozzolino would a sociological institutionalist (in the line of Dembski and Salet) most plausibly make?",o:["What emerges spontaneously mostly raises exchange value for owners, not use value for the residents","Without central evaluation of outcomes, nobody can tell whether the neighbourhood meets its stated goals","Freeing rules and ownership changes little if residents and professionals keep their embedded norms","Each owner will free-ride on shared goods, so a public skeleton must be enforced through coercion"],a:2,e:"Sociological institutionalism stresses informal rules, norms and meanings embedded in mental maps: formal rule changes do not work unless new meanings are inculcated. The first option is the Marxist critique from Tutorial 1, the second a governmental-model complaint and the fourth a rational choice (Olson) argument."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"nuance",q:"Which paraphrase states Cozzolino's central claim most precisely?",o:["AANs should be demolished and replaced by neighbourhoods that copy the form of traditional towns","AANs fail mainly because their residents lack the will to improve and change their surroundings","The main AAN problem is less a lack of central plans than the lack of room for incremental adaptation","Top-down design is never legitimate, not even for main infrastructure and public spaces in new areas"],a:2,e:"Cozzolino: 'the main problem with AANs is not so much... the need for further centrally coordinated plans, investments, and interventions, but rather the lack of flexible space for incremental adaptation and the evolution of self-organized orders'. He rejects copying traditional form, says he does not condemn AANs, and accepts top-down design of a public 'skeleton'."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"excerpt",q:"A development framework states: 'The municipality designs and builds the main streets, utilities and a few squares. Within that frame, plots are sold to households and small builders, who may later add floors or change uses as long as they cause no nuisance to their neighbours.' Which concept grounds this framework?",o:["Prescriptive planning that predefines the spatial outcome of every plot in a single master plan","A robust public skeleton designed top-down, with bottom-up change on lots under proscriptive rules","A comprehensive synthesis in which expert planners integrate all relevant dimensions in one view","A legible, simplified order designed from above and laid out over the whole city by the central state"],a:1,e:"This is Cozzolino's recommendation for new areas: think of public spaces 'as a robust skeleton on which further... unpredictable private transformations can occur', with top-down design for common structures and bottom-up design on individual lots, governed by rules that only prevent externalities. The first option is the modern planning he criticises; the others are Rydin's governmental model and Scott's high modernism."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs how Cozzolino explains the lock-in of AANs?",o:["Built at once by one designer, with rules that fix the form and communal ownership; few actors can change anything; poverty then drives residents away, so the market renews the area","Built at once by one designer, with rules that fix the form and many small owners; so many actors adapt freely, and the area keeps growing in complexity and stays attractive","Built at once by one designer, with rules that fix the form and communal ownership; few actors can make small changes, so no self-renewal; decline invites top-down regeneration","Built at once by one designer, with rules that fix the form and communal ownership; so residents organise and renew the area through collective action without outside help"],a:2,e:"Concentrated design control, frozen rules and communal ownership leave little room for spontaneous action, so AANs cannot self-regenerate; decline then calls for top-down interventions, creating 'questionable paternalistic circles'. The other chains break a link: many independent owners is the adaptive case, and Cozzolino says AANs are 'unable to self-regenerate', so neither the market nor collective action renews them by default."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"except",q:"Which statement is NOT consistent with Cozzolino (2020)?",o:["New neighbourhoods should always be built slowly, over a long period, to stay adaptable","Adaptability matters for the persistence, identity and innovation of the built environment","The codes of the traditional city aimed to ensure minimum damage to neighbouring owners","Self-building was only 10 to 15% of new housing in 2015, even in the Netherlands and the UK"],a:0,e:"Including time 'does not mean that neighbourhoods should always be developed slowly over a long period of time (although this may be a valid option)'; it means leaving room for progressive, unpredictable adaptation after construction. The other three statements are accurate paraphrases of the article."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"case",q:"A municipality considers four rules for a new district. Which one is a proscriptive rule in Cozzolino's sense?",o:["Each dwelling must come with at least 1.5 parking places on its own plot","No activity on a plot may cause noise above a set level at its boundary","Buildings must follow the facade line and have exactly three storeys","Each block must keep at least 30 per cent of its area as public green"],a:1,e:"Proscriptive rules are prohibitions ('thou shalt not') that only prevent negative externalities on others, such as pollution or noise, without predefining any spatial configuration. The parking, facade and green-space rules are prescriptions of the kind Cozzolino says would block recreating the traditional city's patterns."}
);
NQ_2.push(
{t:"cozz",fmt:"example",q:"Which pair of Bergamo neighbourhoods does Cozzolino use to illustrate his argument?",o:["Borgo Santa Caterina as an AAN and La Clementina as a historic quarter","La Clementina as an AAN and Borgo Santa Caterina as an adaptive quarter","Oosterwold as an AAN and La Clementina as an adaptive new district","The Bijlmermeer as an AAN and Borgo Santa Caterina as a new district"],a:1,e:"La Clementina, built in the 1980s, is his anti-adaptive example; Borgo Santa Caterina is the historic neighbourhood 'subject to a continuous process of (micro) change and renewal'. Oosterwold (Almere) is cited as an experiment in self-organisation and the Bijlmermeer only in a note."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"recall",q:"Cozzolino gives three reasons why adaptability matters in the built environment. Which are they?",o:["Efficiency, equity and sustainability","Density, mixed use and accessibility","Persistence, identity and innovation","Safety, beauty and affordability"],a:2,e:"An adaptive environment persists in the long run (Habraken: renewal 'preserves it'), supports unique identities, and favours innovation by using dispersed local knowledge (Hayek 1945; Jacobs). The other triads are generic planning goals, not his reasons."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"define",q:"What does Cozzolino, following Jacobs, mean by 'locality knowledge'?",o:["Expert knowledge of local conditions collected in surveys","People's own perceptions of their local situation, needs and means","The historic knowledge that is stored in a neighbourhood's archives","Knowledge that planners gain from comparing many neighbourhoods"],a:1,e:"Locality knowledge is the 'subjective perceptions of the local situation of people, depending on individual practical needs, preferences, and means'. The planner's task is to let people use it while avoiding undesirable externalities, not to collect it centrally."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"example",q:"Cozzolino links the AAN characteristic of large open spaces managed by one agency to which problem described by Jacobs?",o:["Synchronous decay of the buildings","The anti-commons of condominiums","Mono-functional dormitory areas","Streets without eyes on them"],a:3,e:"Excessive open public space 'often generates the same problems of streets without eyes' and raises maintenance and control problems. Synchronous decay belongs to construction all at once, the anti-commons to communal ownership, dormitories to functions."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"inverse",q:"With which AAN characteristic does Cozzolino associate 'synchronous processes of degradation'?",o:["Construction all at once, from scratch","Communal ownership in big condominiums","Large open spaces run by a single agency","Rules that fix land uses and functions"],a:0,e:"Because AANs are built all at once, their buildings age and degrade together; incrementally built areas have buildings of different values and maintenance states, which reduces this risk and offers a more differentiated supply."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"not",q:"Which is NOT a merit of AANs that Cozzolino acknowledges?",o:["They responded to great housing pressures, for example during economic booms","They helped cope with urban emergencies such as earthquakes and floods","They let many families become homeowners at an affordable price","They keep their attractiveness to residents and visitors over time"],a:3,e:"Cozzolino credits AANs with meeting housing pressure, coping with emergencies and enabling affordable ownership. Attractiveness is the opposite: high at first but decreasing 'exponentially in the long-run'."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"apply",q:"A municipality wants to revive an isolated post-war estate and plans a new self-sufficient service centre inside it. Following Cozzolino, what should it rather prioritise?",o:["A new master plan fixing the estate's future uses and densities","Better accessibility and connections to better-equipped areas","A single developer to rebuild the estate in one coordinated phase","Larger public open spaces to raise the estate's attractiveness"],a:1,e:"Cozzolino says planners can help by improving 'accessibility and connection to better equipped urban areas' and should abandon the original ideal of self-sufficient neighbourhood units: AANs need continuous exchange with their surroundings. The other options reproduce AAN characteristics."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"contrast",q:"How does Cozzolino distance himself from movements such as New Urbanism?",o:["He rejects any role for the traditional city in today's planning debates","He argues that traditional urban form should be copied in new districts","He focuses on the institutions that shaped how the city evolved","He holds that only public authorities should design neighbourhoods today"],a:2,e:"He is not nostalgic: 'the focus... concerns not only the physical configuration of the traditional city but, more importantly, the institutional framework that shaped its evolutionary process', which marks his distance from New Urbanism. He even says emulating traditional form cannot reproduce its complexity."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"recall",q:"How does Cozzolino summarise the founding principles of the traditional city?",o:["Comprehensive plans, public land banking and strict design codes","Private ownership, incremental growth and organic adjustment","Communal ownership, self-sufficient units and mixed land uses","Royal decrees, uniform facades and wide ceremonial boulevards"],a:1,e:"'The founding principles of the traditional city can be summed up by private ownership, incremental development, and organic adjustments among different households', within codes that protected neighbours. These allowed both respect for the existing fabric and openness to novelty."},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"define",q:"Cozzolino calls adaptability 'a relative concept'. What does he mean?",o:["A neighbourhood is adaptive only if it has all eight characteristics","Adaptability depends on how residents feel about their area today","A place can be more or less adaptive than another, but not absolutely so","Adaptability can only be judged after a neighbourhood is demolished"],a:2,e:"The characteristics in Figure 3 appear 'to different degrees and levels': one neighbourhood can be more or less adaptive than another 'but cannot be adaptive (or anti-adaptive) in absolute terms', and not all characteristics need to be present."}
);
OQ_2.push(
{t:"cozz",fmt:"excerpt",pts:15,q:"Excerpt from Cozzolino (2020): 'Indeed, the main problem with AANs is not so much, as many planners and policy makers largely believe, the need for further centrally coordinated plans, investments, and interventions, but rather the lack of flexible space for incremental adaptation and the evolution of self-organized orders.' (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this argument? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the article. (10)",model:"(a) A liberal, complexity-oriented perspective in the Hayek and Moroni line (evolutionary institutionalism / spontaneous order): good urban order emerges from many decentralised actions within general rules rather than from comprehensive design.\n(b) Argument 1, emergent versus designed order: Cozzolino contrasts a simple, 'prevalently artificial order that is, by definition, designed' with complex order that is 'predominantly the result of spontaneous action', and calls it a trade-off: 'the more one neighbourhood is designed from the top, the less space there is for complexity to evolve'. This mirrors Hayek's spontaneous order (kosmos) versus designed order (taxis), as in Moroni. Argument 2, dispersed knowledge and limited foresight: adaptability 'welcomes innovation by making more efficient use of so-called dispersed local knowledge (Hayek, 1945; Jacobs, 1961)', and rules should not impose 'a stringent technocratic order' because 'we cannot predict all future practical needs for adaptation'. Hence his preference for proscriptive rules that only prevent externalities and for 'multiple independent ownerships'. Both arguments place agency ('action (as the main source of urban change)') and institutional rules that enable it at the centre, which is why Tutorial 1 links him to Hayek and to the Free City, with its normative preference for autonomy, distributed control and anti-paternalism.",rubric:["(a) Names liberal / complexity / spontaneous-order (Hayek-Moroni, evolutionary) perspective","Argument 1 on emergent vs designed order with a verbatim quote","Argument 2 on dispersed/locality knowledge or limits of prediction with a verbatim quote","Explains the link between quotes and perspective (e.g. kosmos/taxis, proscriptive rules)","Avoids misreading him as nostalgic or anti-planning altogether"]},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"position",pts:10,q:"Where does Cozzolino (2020) sit on the structure–agency dimension? Justify your position with the text, and give one criticism that follows from this position.",model:"Cozzolino leans towards agency, while recognising that institutions enable or constrain it (the Tutorial 1 reading). He names 'action (as the main source of urban change)' as one of his three neglected factors, quotes Habraken that the built environment is complex 'by virtue of human actions', and explains adaptability through 'the number and intensity of spontaneous actions' of dwellers who convert flats into offices or add floors. Yet he gives institutions a decisive role: ownership is 'the legal title that effectively distributes design control', rules can 'freeze' adaptation, and his focus is 'the institutional framework that shaped [the traditional city's] evolutionary process'. Structures here are mainly enabling frameworks, not deep economic forces.\nCriticism (Tutorial 1, from a Marxist angle): 'actors are not equally capable of acting'. Tenants in a corporation-owned estate cannot adapt their homes, and 'many independent owners' presumes ownership. Markets are a power structure, so what emerges 'spontaneously' may enhance exchange value for owners rather than use value for residents. An agency-centred account thus underplays how capital and property relations shape who gets to act.",rubric:["Places him towards agency with institutions as enabling/constraining","At least two textual supports (action as main source of change; ownership/rules; institutional framework)","Gives a criticism logically following from the agency emphasis (unequal capacity, tenants, markets as power)","Names the perspective behind the criticism (Marxist/political economy)"]},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"apply",pts:10,q:"Hypothetical: a Dutch municipality must regenerate a 1970s high-rise estate (2,000 flats, one housing corporation, large collective green space, only housing). The alderman proposes a new master plan with partial demolition and a new shopping centre, designed by one firm and built in one phase. Advise the alderman using Cozzolino's concepts, and mention one limitation of his approach in this case.",model:"Cozzolino would warn that the proposal reproduces the AAN logic: one designer, all-at-once construction and centrally coordinated investment, the 'paternalistic circle' in which an area that cannot self-regenerate receives another top-down intervention. The estate shows the AAN characteristics: large scale, mono-functionality, a big open space managed by one agency and concentrated ownership. His advice: break the circle by enlarging room for local experimentation. Change rules so they no longer coincide with the built form (allow demolition and rebuilding, extra floor area, a plurality of uses), but recognise that rule change alone is insufficient when ownership concentrates design control: split ownership (sell plots or ground floors, allow cooperatives or self-build on parts of the green space) to distribute design control. Improve connections to better-equipped areas instead of creating a self-sufficient centre. Keep top-down design for a 'robust skeleton' of streets and essential public space only, and include time: phase changes so future, unpredictable adaptations remain possible.\nLimitation: most residents are tenants with little capacity to act; distributing ownership may displace them or mainly raise exchange value (the Marxist critique), so affordability safeguards are needed that Cozzolino does not discuss.",rubric:["Diagnoses the proposal as reproducing AAN features / paternalistic circle","Recommends rule changes AND ownership distribution, noting rules alone may fail","Mentions connections, public skeleton or time","Identifies a relevant limitation (tenants, displacement, exchange value)"]},
{t:"cozz",fmt:"compare",pts:10,q:"Compare Cozzolino (2020) with Scott's account of the high-modernist city on one point: why top-down designed neighbourhoods fail.",model:"Both criticise neighbourhoods designed from above and both draw on Jane Jacobs. Scott explains failure through the high-modernist ideology of a strong state (Le Corbusier, CIAM, Brasília, Chandigarh): planners imposed a legible, geometric, functionally segregated order seen from above, which produced visual order but social failure and an unplanned city at the margins, because real urban order is complex and experienced from the street. His emphasis is on the state's simplifying view and the practical knowledge it ignores.\nCozzolino explains failure institutionally and over time. AANs are not just badly designed; they are locked in because design control is concentrated (one designer, communal ownership), construction happens all at once, and prescriptive rules coincide with the built form, so no one can make the small adjustments that keep an area adaptive. His remedy is not better design but proscriptive rules, distributed ownership and room for incremental change. The key difference: Scott diagnoses an authoritarian, legibility-seeking state, while Cozzolino points to ordinary planning rules and ownership patterns, also in democratic and private developments (big condominiums), and stresses time.",rubric:["Shared ground: critique of top-down design, Jacobs","Scott: high-modernist ideology, legibility, view from above, social failure","Cozzolino: lock-in via design control, ownership, prescriptive rules, time","States a clear difference (state ideology vs institutional rules/ownership; time dimension)"]}
);
/* ===== gov: Rydin (2021) governmental models ===== */
HQ_2.push(
{t:"gov",fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: In the governmental model, the involvement of stakeholders is the main source of a plan's legitimacy. Statement II: Rydin writes that the legitimacy of plans and planning decisions comes from the rationality of the analysis and propositions put forward by planners.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:3,e:"Rydin says societal preferences and stakeholder engagement 'may be an important input into planning, but the legitimacy of plans and planning decisions comes from the rationality of the analysis'; the ideal type involves stakeholders 'only in an instrumental way to increase effectiveness'. So I is false and II is true."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Implementation studies have questioned the clear divide between the political and the administrative that the governmental model assumes. Statement II: They found that policy goals are reformulated during implementation, so that planners as administrators are inevitably political.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:0,e:"Rydin: research suggested 'policy formulation and implementation are closely entangled with policy goals being reformulated through the implementation process so that planners, as administrators, are inevitably political'. That finding is exactly why the Weberian line between politicians (ends) and administrators (means) blurs."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"combo",q:"According to Rydin's conclusion, which features characterise research done within the governmental frame? I. The goals of planning are taken as given. II. It typically becomes a form of policy evaluation, with data collected before and after the planning action. III. It locates the causes of planning failure in the wider context of markets, power and politics.",o:["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],a:0,e:"Research in this frame 'typically becomes a form of policy evaluation' in which 'the goals are taken as given', with before-and-after data: 'a critical friend shadowing the steps'. III is the opposite: problems are seen to lie within the planning process, and the frame does not stray far into the context in which planning operates."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"case",q:"Hypothetical: a province asks researchers whether its regional housing plan met its stated targets (12,000 homes, 30% social rent) by 2025, comparing goals with outcomes per municipality. Which concept from Rydin's chapter describes this research most precisely?",o:["Planning implementation in Smith's sense, a process-based evaluation of how the plan was integrated","Plan implementation in Smith's sense, judging outcomes against the plan's own stated objectives","Kaza's view that plans should be judged by how they framed debates rather than by their outcomes","Disjointed incrementalism, in which progress is made through a series of small corrective steps"],a:1,e:"Smith (2018) calls evaluation against stated goals 'plan implementation', as opposed to 'planning implementation', which is closer to integration and needs separate process-based evaluation. The study compares targets and outcomes only, so it is plan implementation; Kaza argues precisely that not every plan should be judged that way."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"case",q:"Hypothetical: a government sets minimum standards for floor space, daylight and insulation in all new homes, because society has decided everyone should have a decent dwelling even if some would not pay for it themselves. In Rydin's welfare-economics justification, decent housing here is best described as…",o:["a public good, since once supplied to some, nobody can be excluded from it","a negative externality, since bad housing harms others outside the market","a monopoly problem, since landowners can withhold land to raise prices","a merit good, which society collectively decides people should have"],a:3,e:"Rydin defines merit goods as 'those things that a society collectively decides it is useful for people to have', with good housing as her example. A public good is non-excludable (street lighting), which housing is not; externalities and monopoly are other sources of market failure."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"critique",q:"Which criticism of the governmental model would a rational choice (public choice) theorist most plausibly make?",o:["Problems such as housing shortages have no definitive formulation, so no optimal solution can be calculated","Officials and politicians pursue their own interests too, so state failure is as likely as market failure","Planning cannot serve a single public interest, since plural interests need advocates for the weak","Planning serves the reproduction of capital, so its claims to neutral rationality conceal class power"],a:1,e:"Rational choice treats all actors, including municipalities and planners, as self-interested, so there is no benevolent planner correcting market failure; planning faces state failure too. The other options are Rittel and Webber's wicked problems, Davidoff's advocacy planning and a Marxist critique."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"nuance",q:"How does Rydin describe the role of tools such as cost-benefit analysis, multi-criteria analysis and GIS in the governmental model?",o:["They identify the optimal option, so politicians only need to ratify what the analysis shows","They are rejected by the model as too technocratic for decisions in which values play a role","They reveal the public interest directly, which makes political goal-setting largely unnecessary","Their proponents present them as offering information to the place where decisions are taken"],a:3,e:"Rydin: proponents 'usually emphasise that these are not decision-making tools but rather ways of offering information to the planning system, where the legitimate locus of taking decisions lies'; the lecture's MKBA slide says the same: it informs, politicians decide. The first and third options overstate the tools' role; the second inverts the model."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"excerpt",q:"A master's thesis states: 'This study evaluates to what extent the municipality's climate adaptation plan achieved its stated objectives and which policy instruments were most effective, in order to recommend improvements to the planning process.' Which frame grounds this thesis, and which signal shows it?",o:["Davoudi's enlightenment model: research gradually shifts how problems are framed","Urban politics: it asks whose interests the plan served and how power shaped it","The governmental model: goals are taken as given and the research question is instrumental","Hayek's liberalism: it asks how far collective action should reach beyond agreement"],a:2,e:"The thesis takes the plan's objectives as given, asks which instruments work best, and locates improvements within the planning process: the signals the lecture listed for spotting the governmental approach ('a critical friend shadowing the steps'). Urban politics would question the goals and the distribution of benefits, which this thesis does not."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs the welfare-economics justification of planning in the governmental model?",o:["Markets fail because planners lack data; so the state collects evidence; the public interest is then whatever the experts find","Markets allocate efficiently except under market failure; so the state may step in; the public interest is the greatest net benefit","Markets always fail to serve the public; so the state replaces them; the public interest is set by planners' own analysis","Markets allocate efficiently except under market failure; so landowners must be compensated; the public interest is property"],a:1,e:"The lecture's chain: markets allocate efficiently, except when they don't (externalities, missing markets, public and merit goods, monopoly), so the state may intervene; the public interest is the greatest net benefit to society and planning becomes welfare optimisation. Goals are set politically, not by planners, and markets are not presumed always to fail."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"except",q:"Which statement is NOT consistent with Rydin's chapter on governmental models?",o:["The model keeps its focus on planning's internal dynamics rather than on the context in which it operates","Given confounding market and social dynamics, the effectiveness of planning tends to be taken for granted","Kaza argues that not all plans should be judged by the extent to which they have actually been implemented","The official Chongming self-assessment, with 19 of 22 targets met, confirmed the researchers' findings"],a:3,e:"Ma et al. developed their broader matrix precisely because official indicator sets (19 of 22 targets met) diverged from qualified assessments; they found weak transport, poor waste sorting and little gain for residents. The other three statements accurately paraphrase Rydin."}
);
NQ_2.push(
{t:"gov",fmt:"recall",q:"In Lichfield's general planning process (Rydin 2021, Fig. 2.1), which step comes directly after 'plan design'?",o:["Testing of alternative plans","Data collection and forecasting","Determination of objectives","Review of developments over time"],a:0,e:"The sequence runs: problem definition, decision to act, data collection and forecasting, constraints and objectives, operational criteria, plan design, testing of alternative plans, plan evaluation, decision-taking, implementation, and review through time."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"example",q:"Rydin illustrates evidence-based planning with major infrastructure such as wind farms. What kind of knowledge does she say such projects require?",o:["Knowledge of public opinion, gathered through deliberative citizens' panels","Knowledge of developers' profits, so that betterment can be taxed fairly","Knowledge of how future generations will value the landscape of the site","Knowledge of impacts such as bird strikes, noise, flicker and harm to fish stocks"],a:3,e:"The planning process needs knowledge of how turbines may affect birds, residents (noise and flicker) and, offshore, fishing fleets, fish and marine mammals. This is knowledge of how a development has impacts, gathered 'in a positive way' to support decisions."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"define",q:"Rydin's chapter calls street lighting 'a classic' example of which kind of good?",o:["A merit good","A public good","A missing market","A monopoly good"],a:1,e:"Public goods are those which, 'once supplied to some, it is not possible to exclude others from having'; street lighting is the classic case. Good housing is her example of a merit good."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"recall",q:"In Rydin's short summary of the governmental ideal type, how are stakeholders involved?",o:["As co-producers who set the goals of the plan","As the main source of the plan's legitimacy","Only instrumentally, to raise effectiveness","Not at all, since experts make all decisions"],a:2,e:"The ideal type 'involves stakeholders but only in an instrumental way to increase effectiveness'. Goals come from the political sphere and legitimacy from the rationality of the planners' analysis; stakeholders are still involved, so 'not at all' overstates."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"example",q:"Hettinga et al.'s Geodesign pilot in Escamp (The Hague) is used by Rydin to illustrate which research theme?",o:["Supporting decision-making with new tools","Evaluating implementation against plan goals","Assessing integration of impact assessments","Explaining power struggles over energy policy"],a:0,e:"The Escamp decision support system, with 12 municipal sustainability officers, a touch table and real-time impact assessment, is one of two examples of 'supporting decision-making'; the other is Kumar et al.'s vulnerability index for Bangalore. Power struggles fall outside the governmental frame."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"inverse",q:"Which study in Rydin's chapter found that integrating many impact assessments into Strategic Environmental Assessment can downgrade some values, especially environmental ones?",o:["Kumar et al. on climate vulnerability in Bangalore","Pinho on a tourism scheme at Ria de Aveiro","Zhou et al. on multi-planning in Yulin City","Tajima and Fischer on English spatial planning"],a:3,e:"Tajima and Fischer (2013) studied integration of impact assessments in English SEA/Sustainability Appraisal and found that integration 'could also lead to the subordination of certain assessment issues'; environmental values were considered less than others. Zhou et al. also concern integration, but of fragmented Chinese planning systems."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"not",q:"Which is NOT among the factors that implementation studies (Smith 2018) identify as shaping how policy tools work?",o:["The number of steps in the planning model used","Appropriateness of the intervention to its context","The availability of resources and power","Communication between the different actors"],a:0,e:"Smith lists appropriateness to the context, compatibility between elements, structural context and scope of agency, resources and power, catalysts, and communication between actors. The insight is that outcomes depend on how tools are used, not on the number of formal steps."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"apply",q:"What does Pinho's study of a tourism development at Ria de Aveiro (Portugal) show, according to Rydin?",o:["That expert EIA commissions reliably produce integrated, balanced decisions","That local plans in Portugal leave little room for bargaining with developers","That discretion and negotiation within separated procedures blocked any balanced view","That a new national government finally approved the project after it was redesigned"],a:2,e:"Pinho followed the paper trail: a scheme supported locally was scaled down through EIA negotiation, then refused after a new national government's commission raised coastal erosion. Planning and EIA operated in complete separation, and parties negotiated on their own interests, which prevented an integrated or balanced approach."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"recall",q:"Why does Rydin see the smart city as a possible latest exemplar of the governmental approach?",o:["It hands decisions over to citizens using digital participation","It replaces public administration with private platform firms","It rejects comprehensive plans in favour of experimentation","It integrates technological rationality into urban planning"],a:3,e:"Rydin: 'The idea of the smart city might be seen as the latest exemplar of this approach with its integration of technological rationality into the heart of urban planning'. Experimentation and complex systems belong to a different chapter of her book."},
{t:"gov",fmt:"example",q:"Which Dutch example did the rational planning lecture use to show the governmental model institutionalised in post-war housing (1945–1965)?",o:["Self-build plots with only a few simple rules in Almere's Oosterwold","Central standards for what a home should be, like standard floor plans","Squatted buildings that were legalised by the Amsterdam municipality","A social cost-benefit analysis of the Westerscheldetunnel toll"],a:1,e:"The slide 'Deciding centrally what a home should be (1945–1965)' shows standard floor plans of the Studiegroep Efficiënte Woningbouw (1952); the reading card adds the ministry's 'Voorschriften en Wenken'. The MKBA is a later example of rational tools, and Oosterwold and squatting belong to other perspectives."}
);
OQ_2.push(
{t:"gov",fmt:"excerpt",pts:15,q:"Excerpt from Rydin (2021): 'To be clear, planning systems and planning actors seek to achieve this comprehensive synthesis rationally; societal preferences as expressed through the political system and public or stakeholder engagement exercises may be an important input into planning, but the legitimacy of plans and planning decisions comes from the rationality of the analysis and propositions that are put forward by planners and planning agencies.' (a) Which model of planning does this passage describe? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a quote from the excerpt or the chapter. (10)",model:"(a) The governmental (public administration) model: rational-comprehensive planning in the Weberian tradition, based on instrumental rationality, in which planning links means to politically given ends.\n(b) Argument 1, separation of ends and means: preferences enter 'through the political system', and the chapter states 'The goals themselves stand outside the planning process; they are set by the political realm'. The public administration then interprets these goals, and its officials 'bring expert knowledge to bear on the options available and how policy instruments may be used'. This is the Weberian division of labour: politicians set ends, trained officials choose means. Argument 2, legitimacy through rational, comprehensive analysis: planners 'seek to achieve this comprehensive synthesis rationally', looking 'across all relevant issues and impacts to produce a reasoned and balanced overall view', and 'the legitimacy of plans and planning decisions comes from the rationality of the analysis'. Stakeholder input is only an 'input', in line with the ideal type that 'involves stakeholders but only in an instrumental way to increase effectiveness'. Knowledge is assumed to be gathered 'in a positive way' (universal, monist, realist on the course axes). Together these show a model whose authority rests on expert rationality, which critics such as Lindblom, Rittel and Webber, and Davidoff challenge.",rubric:["(a) Names the governmental / rational-comprehensive (public administration, Weberian) model","Argument 1: politically set goals vs expert-chosen means, with a verbatim quote","Argument 2: legitimacy from rational, comprehensive analysis; stakeholders instrumental, with a verbatim quote","Explains how each quote shows the model","Optional depth: knowledge assumptions or a critic, accurately attributed"]},
{t:"gov",fmt:"short",pts:5,q:"Explain what Rydin means when she says research within the governmental frame becomes 'a critical friend shadowing the steps set out in the rational planning model'.",model:"Research in this frame mirrors the model it studies. It takes the goals of planning as given, as the rational planner does, and follows the steps of the process (data, alternatives, decision, implementation, monitoring) to see how instruments were used and what worked or did not, using data before and after the planning action. It is critical, because it identifies failures and suggests improvements, often new tools such as GIS or ICT-supported engagement, but it is a friend, because it does not question the model, the goals or the planner's central role. Problems and solutions are located inside the planning process, not in markets, power or politics, and evaluation results become evidence that feeds back into planning.",rubric:["Goals taken as given / research mirrors the model","Focus on steps, instruments, before-after evaluation","Explains 'critical' (finds failures) and 'friend' (does not question model)","Notes that context (markets, power) is left out"]},
{t:"gov",fmt:"commentary",pts:15,q:"An article on a Dutch road project argues: 'The social cost-benefit analysis shows a benefit-cost ratio of 1.5. Since all relevant effects have been weighed in one transparent framework, the rational choice is clear and the political debate can be closed.' (a) Identify the perspective grounding this argument. (b) Critique it from an alternative course perspective of your choice. (c) What would that alternative perspective recommend instead?",model:"(a) The argument rests on the governmental, rational-comprehensive model (Rydin 2021): welfare economics defines the public interest as the greatest net benefit, a comprehensive synthesis weighs all effects in one framework (the MKBA expresses effects 'as much as possible in euros'), and legitimacy is claimed from 'the rationality of the analysis'. It even overstates that model, since Rydin notes that proponents present such tools as informing, not making, decisions.\n(b) Critique from Davoudi's post-positivist view of evidence (with Rittel and Webber): evidence is never a neutral, complete input. Framing choices decide what counts as an effect, and some effects cannot be priced: in the lecture's Westerscheldetunnel MKBA 'the feeling of injustice about the toll' could not be put in euros. In policy, evidence competes with ideology, interests and institutions, and the planning problem itself may be wicked: no definitive formulation, solutions good or bad rather than true or false. Treating a ratio as closing debate turns a value choice into a technical one and hides whose costs and benefits are counted.\n(c) Davoudi would recommend an enlightenment, evidence-informed approach: use the MKBA as one input that frames and informs debate, make its assumptions and unpriced effects explicit, combine it with other knowledge (including local and qualitative evidence), and leave the decision to open political deliberation in which planners acknowledge uncertainty.",rubric:["(a) Identifies the governmental/rational model with key features (welfare economics, comprehensive synthesis, legitimacy via analysis)","(b) Critique logically follows from the chosen perspective's assumptions (e.g. evidence not neutral, wicked problems, plural interests)","(b) Uses a concrete example or concept (unpriced effects, Westerschelde, framing)","(c) Gives a recommendation consistent with the chosen perspective","Notes that even Rydin's model sees tools as informing, not deciding"]},
{t:"gov",fmt:"compare",pts:10,q:"Compare Rydin's governmental model (2021) with Hayek's 'Planning and democracy' (1944) on one point: whether ends can be set politically and the choice of means left to experts.",model:"The governmental model is built on this separation. In the Weberian tradition 'the goals themselves stand outside the planning process; they are set by the political realm', and the administration brings 'expert knowledge to bear on the options available'. Planning is instrumental rationality: choosing the best means for given ends, with legitimacy from the rationality of the analysis, and goals are 'taken as given'.\nHayek denies that this separation can hold for comprehensive planning. People can agree that there should be a plan, but 'agreement will in fact exist only on the mechanism', because no 'complete ethical code' ranks all ends. The ends of a plan 'cannot be defined apart from the particular plan', so experts who work out the plan must choose 'between conflicting or competing ends' and will 'impose their scale of preferences on the community'. Delegating means is harmless only where parliament has 'first agreed on the aim', like a general with one supreme goal. Interestingly, implementation studies in Rydin's chapter reach a related conclusion from inside the model: goals are reformulated during implementation, so planners are 'inevitably political'. The difference is the remedy: Rydin's model seeks better process and tools, while Hayek wants to limit collective action to fields of true agreement and bind power by general rules.",rubric:["Governmental model: Weberian separation, goals outside the process, experts choose means","Hayek: no agreement on ends, ends not definable apart from the plan, experts impose preferences","Notes Hayek's exception (delegation fine when aim agreed / general's single goal)","Draws a link or contrast in remedies (implementation studies; better tools vs limiting scope)"]}
);
